<% content_for :title, "Chart — Shadwire" %>
<% content_for :description, t("components.pages.chart.subtitle") %>
<% content_for :head do %>
  <%= tag.style rouge_stylesheet %>
<% end %>

<article class="space-y-12">
  <header class="space-y-3">
    <h1 class="text-4xl font-bold tracking-[-0.01em] leading-[0.92] sm:text-5xl">Chart</h1>
    <p class="text-lg leading-snug text-muted-foreground"><%= t("components.pages.chart.subtitle") %></p>
  </header>

  <%= ui_card(class: "p-6", data: { docs_toc_skip: "" }) do %>
    <%= render "components/examples/chart_area" %>
  <% end %>

  <%= render "components/install", name: "chart" %>

  <section class="space-y-4">
    <%= docs_code_block(@snippets[:importmap], language: :ruby) %>
  </section>

  <section class="space-y-4">
    <h2 class="border-b pb-2 text-2xl font-bold tracking-[-0.01em]">How a chart is put together</h2>
    <p class="leading-7 text-muted-foreground">
      A chart is a container with parts inside it. The container, <%= docs_code("ui_chart") %>,
      holds what all the parts share: the rows, the config and the layout. Each part, such as
      <%= docs_code("ui_chart_grid") %>, <%= docs_code("ui_chart_x_axis") %>,
      <%= docs_code("ui_chart_bar") %> or <%= docs_code("ui_chart_tooltip") %>, draws one layer, and
      the layers stack in the order you write them, like the children of a Recharts chart. You don't
      pick a chart type: put bars and a line in the same container and you get a combined chart. Plain
      HTML inside the container is drawn on top.
    </p>
    <p class="leading-7 text-muted-foreground">
      D3 does the drawing. The container's Stimulus controller, <%= docs_code("ui-chart") %>, measures
      the chart, computes the scales and stacks, and passes them to every part in a
      <%= docs_code("ui-chart:render") %> event. Each part has its own controller that draws its own
      <%= docs_code("<svg>") %> with D3. That means you can delete the files of parts you don't use,
      and write new parts when you need them. Colors are CSS custom properties, so switching the theme
      recolors the chart without redrawing it. D3 is only loaded on pages that have a chart.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="space-y-4">
    <h2 class="border-b pb-2 text-2xl font-bold tracking-[-0.01em]">Your first chart</h2>
    <p class="leading-7 text-muted-foreground">
      This section builds a bar chart, then adds a grid, an axis, a tooltip and a legend, one part at
      a time.
    </p>

    <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold tracking-[-0.006em]">Start with the rows</h3>
    <p class="leading-7 text-muted-foreground">
      One hash per category, with whatever keys you like; each part's <%= docs_code("data_key:") %>
      says which field it reads. The rows usually come from a query. Dates and times are sent as ISO
      strings, which the axes and the tooltip format for you, and a <%= docs_code("BigDecimal") %>
      arrives as a string and is read as a number.
    </p>
    <%= docs_code_block(@snippets[:rows], language: :ruby) %>

    <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold tracking-[-0.006em]">Define the config</h3>
    <p class="leading-7 text-muted-foreground">
      The config holds what the rows don't: each series' label and color. It is separate from the
      data so that several charts can share one config, and so the rows can come from anywhere.
    </p>
    <%= docs_code_block(@snippets[:config], language: :ruby) %>

    <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold tracking-[-0.006em]">Compose the chart</h3>
    <p class="leading-7 text-muted-foreground">
      Add one <%= docs_code("ui_chart_bar") %> per series. The container is
      <%= docs_code("aspect-video") %> by default; also give it a <%= docs_code("min-h-*") %> or a
      height, or it has no room to draw.
    </p>
    <%= docs_code_block(@snippets[:bars]) %>

    <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold tracking-[-0.006em]">Add a grid</h3>
    <p class="leading-7 text-muted-foreground">
      <%= docs_code("ui_chart_grid") %> draws a line at each tick of the value axis.
    </p>
    <%= docs_code_block(@snippets[:grid]) %>

    <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold tracking-[-0.006em]">Add an axis</h3>
    <p class="leading-7 text-muted-foreground">
      The x axis labels the categories with the <%= docs_code("data_key:") %> field. The months are
      dates, so <%= docs_code("tick_format:") %> is a d3-time-format specifier here, and the labels
      read <em>Jan</em>, <em>Feb</em>… in the page's language.
    </p>
    <%= docs_code_block(@snippets[:axis]) %>

    <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold tracking-[-0.006em]">Add a tooltip</h3>
    <p class="leading-7 text-muted-foreground">
      Hover over a month and <%= docs_code("ui_chart_tooltip") %> lists each series' value for that
      month, under the month's full name.
    </p>
    <%= docs_code_block(@snippets[:tooltip]) %>

    <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold tracking-[-0.006em]">Add a legend</h3>
    <p class="leading-7 text-muted-foreground">
      <%= docs_code("ui_chart_legend") %> shows the series with the labels from the config, below the
      plot.
    </p>
    <%= docs_code_block(@snippets[:legend]) %>

    <%= render "components/example", **@first_chart %>
  </section>

  <section class="space-y-4">
    <h2 class="border-b pb-2 text-2xl font-bold tracking-[-0.01em]"><%= t("components.sections.composition") %></h2>
    <%= docs_code_block(@snippets[:composition], language: :text) %>
  </section>

  <section class="space-y-4">
    <h2 class="border-b pb-2 text-2xl font-bold tracking-[-0.01em]">Chart config</h2>
    <p class="leading-7 text-muted-foreground">
      Each key in the config is a series, or a category that a pie, a ring or a legend shows. It takes
      a <%= docs_code("label:") %>, either a <%= docs_code("color:") %> or a <%= docs_code("theme:") %>
      with one color per theme, and optionally a Lucide <%= docs_code("icon:") %>, which the legend and
      the tooltip show instead of the color swatch.
    </p>
    <%= docs_code_block(@snippets[:config_full], language: :ruby) %>
  </section>

  <section class="space-y-4">
    <h2 class="border-b pb-2 text-2xl font-bold tracking-[-0.01em]">Theming</h2>
    <p class="leading-7 text-muted-foreground">
      Each color in the config becomes a <%= docs_code("--color-<key>") %> custom property on the
      chart, and every part draws with those. Set them to the theme's chart tokens and the chart
      follows the theme, including dark mode.
    </p>
    <%= docs_code_block(@snippets[:theme_tokens], language: :css) %>
    <p class="leading-7 text-muted-foreground">
      Any CSS color works too (hex, <%= docs_code("hsl()") %>, <%= docs_code("oklch()") %>). The
      variables are not limited to the parts: a part's own <%= docs_code("color:") %>, a row's
      <%= docs_code("fill") %> and your own markup inside the chart can use them as well.
    </p>
    <%= docs_code_block(@snippets[:colors_elsewhere]) %>
  </section>

  <section class="space-y-4">
    <h2 class="border-b pb-2 text-2xl font-bold tracking-[-0.01em]">Tooltip</h2>
    <p class="leading-7 text-muted-foreground">
      The tooltip shows a label (the category) and one row per series, with an indicator, a name and a
      value. <%= docs_code("indicator:") %> is <%= docs_code(":dot") %>, <%= docs_code(":line") %> or
      <%= docs_code(":dashed") %>; <%= docs_code("hide_label:") %> and <%= docs_code("hide_indicator:") %>
      hide those. <%= docs_code("cursor:") %> shades the category under the pointer, and
      <%= docs_code("default_index:") %> shows the tooltip for a category before anyone hovers.
    </p>
    <p class="leading-7 text-muted-foreground">
      <%= docs_code("label_key:") %> and <%= docs_code("name_key:") %> take the label and the names from
      another config entry or field. Here the label is <em>Total visitors</em> and each name is the
      browser the slice represents:
    </p>
    <%= docs_code_block(@snippets[:tooltip_keys]) %>
  </section>

  <section class="space-y-4">
    <h2 class="border-b pb-2 text-2xl font-bold tracking-[-0.01em]">Legend</h2>
    <p class="leading-7 text-muted-foreground">
      The legend lists the series (or, for a pie, its slices) with the labels and colors from the
      config. With <%= docs_code("name_key:") %> it lists the rows instead, named by that field, and
      <%= docs_code("vertical_align: :top") %> moves it above the plot.
    </p>
    <%= docs_code_block(@snippets[:legend_name_key]) %>
  </section>

  <section class="space-y-4">
    <h2 class="border-b pb-2 text-2xl font-bold tracking-[-0.01em]">Formatting</h2>
    <p class="leading-7 text-muted-foreground">
      The axes' <%= docs_code("tick_format:") %>, the tooltip's <%= docs_code("label_format:") %> and
      <%= docs_code("value_format:") %> and the label lists' <%= docs_code("format:") %> are D3
      specifiers: d3-format for numbers, d3-time-format for dates. Without one, numbers are formatted
      for the page's language.
    </p>
    <%= docs_code_block(@snippets[:formats]) %>
    <p class="leading-7 text-muted-foreground">
      Month and day names and number separators come from the app's own Rails translations
      (<%= docs_code("date.month_names") %>, <%= docs_code("date.abbr_day_names") %>,
      <%= docs_code("number.format.delimiter") %> and so on), so an app in Portuguese gets
      <em>Fev</em> and <em>1.234,5</em> without configuring the chart.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="space-y-4">
    <h2 class="border-b pb-2 text-2xl font-bold tracking-[-0.01em]">A layer of your own</h2>
    <p class="leading-7 text-muted-foreground">
      The built-in parts work the same way as one you write yourself. <%= docs_code("ui_chart_layer") %>
      adds an <%= docs_code("<svg>") %> over the chart and connects it to your own Stimulus controller,
      which receives the chart's scales and draws with D3. This one draws a goal line:
    </p>
    <%= docs_code_block(@snippets[:layer]) %>
    <%= docs_code_block(@snippets[:layer_controller], language: :javascript) %>
    <p class="leading-7 text-muted-foreground">
      A part receives three events, in this order. <%= docs_code("event.detail.chart") %> contains the
      rows, the config, the plot area, the category and value scales, the polar geometry, a formatter
      and <%= docs_code("d3") %> itself.
    </p>
    <%= docs_code_block(@snippets[:events], language: :text) %>
  </section>

  <section class="space-y-4">
    <h2 class="border-b pb-2 text-2xl font-bold tracking-[-0.01em]">Accessibility</h2>
    <p class="leading-7 text-muted-foreground">
      <%= docs_code("label:") %> gives the chart its accessible name. The drawing is hidden from
      assistive technology, and the legend is plain text. When the chart has a tooltip, it can take
      focus: the arrow keys, <kbd>Home</kbd> and <kbd>End</kbd> move between categories,
      <kbd>Escape</kbd> closes the tooltip, and screen readers announce what it shows. If the user
      prefers reduced motion, nothing animates.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="space-y-8">
    <h2 class="border-b pb-2 text-2xl font-bold tracking-[-0.01em]"><%= t("components.sections.examples") %></h2>
    <% @examples.each do |example| %>
      <%= render "components/example", **example %>
    <% end %>
  </section>

  <%= render "components/api", name: "chart" %>
</article>
